<div class="container page-pad">
  <div class="sec-head rv">
    <div class="l">
      <p class="klabel">About</p>
      <h2>Business information you can actually verify</h2>
      <p>FirmLedger exists for one reason: business information on the web is fragmented, self-reported and rarely verifiable. We're building the trusted place where anyone can find, verify, enrich and track business entities — the business record layer for modern discovery.</p>
    </div>
    <span class="idx">ABOUT</span>
  </div>

  <h3 class="h-section">What a FirmLedger record guarantees</h3>
  <div class="feature-grid" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="feature rv"><h3>Structure</h3><p>Every entity — company, startup, agency, organization, product, service or publisher — follows a strict schema with typed fields, categories and locations, deduplicated into one canonical record.</p></div>
    <div class="feature rv" style="--d:60ms"><h3>Provenance</h3><p>Records carry their sources, a confidence score and a last-verified date. You can see exactly why you should trust a field — and when to re-check it.</p></div>
    <div class="feature rv" style="--d:120ms"><h3>Verified ownership</h3><p>Owners claim profiles by proving control of the business domain — DNS TXT record, HTML meta tag, or the FirmLedger badge — cryptographically verified by our servers.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="h-section" id="sources">How the ledger is built</h3>
  <div class="pipe" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="st"><span class="n">L1</span><h3>Ingest</h3><p>Records arrive from open registries, encyclopedias, news (GDELT), crawls — and public submissions.</p></div>
    <div class="st"><span class="n">L2</span><h3>Normalize</h3><p>Names, categories and fields are standardized into one schema; categories are deduplicated.</p></div>
    <div class="st"><span class="n">L3</span><h3>Resolve</h3><p>Entity resolution merges duplicates into a single canonical record per real-world entity.</p></div>
    <div class="st"><span class="n">L4</span><h3>Verify</h3><p>Confidence scoring plus manual moderation; ownership claims are verified against DNS, meta tags or badges.</p></div>
    <div class="st"><span class="n">L5</span><h3>Publish</h3><p>Approved records publish to the directory, sitemap and RSS, and ping search engines for indexing within ~10h.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="h-section">The verification methods</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table class="table">
      <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What you add</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>DNS record</strong></td><td>TXT <code>firmledger-verification=&lt;token&gt;</code></td><td>Your domain's DNS zone</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Meta tag</strong></td><td><code>&lt;meta name="firmledger-verification" content="&lt;token&gt;"&gt;</code></td><td>Your homepage <code>&lt;head&gt;</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Badge</strong></td><td>The snippet we generate for your listing</td><td>Anywhere on your homepage</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px;font-size:14px">Verification is automated and usually completes in seconds once your record is in place. Re-checks can happen at any time afterwards — removing the proof can revert a profile to unclaimed.</p>

  <h3 class="h-section" id="mission" style="margin-top:44px">The mission</h3>
  <div class="prose rv" style="max-width:820px;font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.8;color:var(--ink2)">
    <p>Business data on the open web is fragmented, self-reported, and nearly impossible to audit. A description that was written by a marketing intern three companies ago sits next to a funding figure from a five-year-old headline, presented with equal confidence. Searchers, clients, investors and job seekers all make decisions on this data — with no visible trail back to any source.</p>
    <p>FirmLedger's mission is to make business information <strong>verifiable by default</strong>. Concretely, that means: one canonical record per entity, never duplicates; every field traceable to a named source you can re-check yourself; cryptographic ownership proofs that tie a profile to the domain of the actual business; automated re-checks with public freshness signals; and a permanent, public removal and correction channel. Everything a computer never saw, a human moderator did — no profile goes live without review.</p>
    <p>We are starting in Kenya — the register we live in and whose business ecosystem we know first-hand — and the schema fits the world. The ledger is open to any entity type, country or industry.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="h-section" id="verification" style="margin-top:44px">How verification works, end to end</h3>
  <div class="prose rv" style="max-width:820px;font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.8;color:var(--ink2)">
    <p>Verification on FirmLedger is a two-stage process. Stage one is <strong>review</strong>: human moderators approve every record submitted to the ledger. Moderators check obvious accuracy (does the website really exist, does the named person plausibly relate to the entity, are fields consistent), then either publish, request changes, or reject. Stage two is <strong>ownership</strong>: after publication, the person who actually controls the business's domain proves that control by DNS record, meta tag, or badge. Once proven, the profile marks “Verified owner”, its confidence weight rises, and editorial control moves to that owner.</p>
    <p>Nothing on a profile — including the “Verified” state itself — is a promise from FirmLedger about the business's conduct. It is a technical reading, displayed transparently with its date and method on every profile, that lets you draw far better conclusions than a bare directory listing ever could.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="feature-grid" style="margin-top:26px">
    <div class="feature rv"><h3>One per entity</h3><p>Same name or same domain, same record — duplicates are impossible by construction. Second submissions redirect to the existing record and offer to claim it.</p></div>
    <div class="feature rv" style="--d:60ms"><h3>Real enrichment only</h3><p>The one-click fetch reads Wikipedia and Wikidata. If a genuine article doesn't exist, the fetch says so and asks you to type the record — it never invents facts.</p></div>
    <div class="feature rv" style="--d:120ms"><h3>Corrections are public</h3><p>Every profile carries a “Request removal” link, and verified owners edit fields directly. Wrong data here gets fixed, not photographed forever.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

